# Published run results ARE meant to be committed -- that is the whole point of
# results_sync.py. Three rules above would otherwise swallow them: `runs/`
# (which matches at any depth, so it hides results/runs/ too), `*.json` (the
# run record and loss history), and `results/mt_eval_results.csv`. The
# directory has to be re-included before the files, because git does not
# descend into an excluded directory to look for negations.
!results/runs/
!results/runs/**

# The auto_setup driver's own state -- its per-step logs, completion markers,
# copied artifacts and manifest -- is mirrored here by lcm_scripts/publish_state.py
# so a pipeline's record survives the machine that ran it. The live state
# directory itself stays ignored under runs/auto_setup, deliberately: pulling
# another machine's `.done` markers into it would make this driver skip steps
# whose checkpoints do not exist here. Same re-include dance as above, for the
# same `runs/`, `*.json` and `*.jsonl` rules.
!results/auto_setup/
!results/auto_setup/**

# Vendored/third-party tooling and downloaded archives (do not commit — they
# bloat the repo). Singularity is a container runtime pulled in for HPC builds.
singularity/
*.tar.gz
go*.linux-amd64.tar.gz
singularity-*.tar.gz
